course: biology
lesson: 231
title: Electric senses: electric eels, sharks, the platypus bill and charged bees
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Every living animal makes tiny electric signals: each heartbeat and each twitch of a muscle sends out a weak electric buzz into the water around it.
explain: We cannot feel these buzzes, but many water animals can. A shark gliding over the sand can feel the heartbeat of a fish hiding underneath, like you finding a friend under a blanket by feeling them breathe.
explain: Some animals go further and make their own electricity: the electric eel stacks thousands of special cells like little batteries in a row and can give a shock strong enough to light a bulb.
explain: Water carries electricity well, which is why almost all electric senses belong to animals that live or hunt in water; on land, bees are a rare surprise.
explain: Our own nerves and heart also run on tiny electric signals, which is why a shock from a socket is so dangerous to us.
fact: Electric eels can give shocks of several hundred volts, about 600 or even more, more than twice the voltage of a home socket in Britain.
fact: Most of an electric eel's long body is made of special cells that work like many small batteries stacked in a row; it is not a true eel but a kind of knifefish, closer to catfish than to true eels.
fact: Electric eels use weak pulses to sense what is around them and strong shocks to stun prey and scare enemies, and even young eels can give a tingle.
fact: Sharks have tiny jelly-filled pores on their snouts, called ampullae of Lorenzini, that feel the weak electricity of a fish's muscles and heartbeat, even when it is buried in sand.
fact: A hammerhead shark's wide head spreads its electric sensors over a wider strip of sea floor, like a metal detector sweeping a beach, as it hunts stingrays hidden under the sand.
fact: A platypus closes its eyes, ears and nose underwater and finds shrimps and worms with the electric and touch sensors in its soft bill.
fact: Elephantnose fish make a weak electric field around themselves and read it with their long chins to find food in dark, muddy African rivers.
fact: Electric rays lie flat on the sand and can stun fish with shocks of up to about 200 volts; Roman doctors once used them to numb pain.
fact: A bumblebee can feel a flower's weak electric charge, which changes for a while after another bee visits, so the bee may skip a flower that was just emptied.
fact: The Guiana dolphin has small pits on its snout, left where its baby whiskers were, that feel electric fields; it was the first dolphin found with this sense, in 2011.
fact: People cannot feel other animals' electric fields, but our hearts and brains work with electric signals that doctors measure with an ECG and an EEG.
fact: A real electric shock makes muscles jump and can stop the heart; it does not just make hair stand up like in cartoons.
exercise: What do a shark's jelly-filled snout pores feel? | Weak electric signals from other animals' muscles and hearts | The smell of blood from a wounded fish many many kilometres away | The temperature of the water | The sound of waves on the shore
exercise: How does a shark find a fish lying still under the sand? | It feels the tiny electricity of the fish's heartbeat and muscles | It sees right through the sand with special eyes that work in the dark | It hears the fish snoring | It smells the sand moving
exercise: Why is a hammerhead's head so wide? | It spreads electric sensors over more sea floor at once | It helps the shark float on the top of the sea like a raft | It is full of extra teeth | It keeps the shark's eyes safe from the sand
exercise: How does a platypus find food underwater with its eyes shut? | Its bill feels the electricity and movement of its prey | It listens carefully for its prey with its ears open wide | It smells the prey through its nose | It waits for food to swim into its mouth
exercise: What makes an electric eel's shock? | Thousands of special cells that work like stacked batteries | A metal stone it swallows | Lightning it catches during storms and keeps inside its body | Friction from rubbing its skin on rocks
exercise: What kind of fish is an electric eel really? | A knifefish, closer to catfish than to true eels | A true eel, just like the ones that swim in our rivers | A small kind of shark | A sea snake with fins
exercise: What are an electric eel's weak pulses for? | Sensing what is around it | Cooking its food before eating it | Talking to the people at the zoo | Keeping its body warm in cold rivers
exercise: What does an elephantnose fish do with its long chin? | It reads its own electric field to find food in the mud | It sucks up water like an elephant's trunk | It sprays water at insects | It scares other fish away by waving it about from side to side
exercise: How does an electric ray catch fish? | It lies on the sand and stuns them with a shock | It chases them at high speed | It spits poison at them from its mouth | It traps them in a sticky net of slime
exercise: What might a bee feel when it lands on a flower another bee has just left? | The flower's changed electric charge | The other bee's tiny footprints on the petals | Hot air left by the first bee | The sound of the first bee's buzz
exercise: What was special about the Guiana dolphin found in 2011? | It was the first dolphin known to feel electric fields | It was the first dolphin ever found with no teeth at all | It could live in fresh ice | It glowed in the dark
exercise: What are the pits on the Guiana dolphin's snout left from? | Its baby whiskers | Old bite marks from sharks | Holes for breathing | Scratches from rocks
exercise: Why do almost all animals with an electric sense live in water? | Water carries electricity much better than air | Water is always warm enough for electric cells to work | There is no light at all down in rivers and seas | Land animals have no muscles or hearts to give off signals
exercise: What does a real electric shock do to a person? | It makes muscles jump and can stop the heart | It only makes your hair stand on end for a while | It makes the person glow in the dark for a while | It does nothing at all to grown-ups, only to children
exercise: Which is true about people and electricity? | Our hearts and brains work with tiny electric signals | People have no electricity in their bodies | People can feel a fish's electricity when they sit in the bath | Only our hair carries electricity
test: «my teacher said people got nothing electric in them at all» What is right? | We can't feel other animals' fields, but our heart and nerves run on electric signals | Right, only fish and eels have electricity | Right, electricity only comes from sockets | Wrong, people can feel a shark from a mile away
test: In a cartoon a cat touches a wire, his fur stands up, he goes black and walks off fine. What is true? | A real shock can stop the heart, so it is dangerous, not funny | Real shocks only make fur fluffy | Cats cannot be hurt by electricity, because their fur protects them like rubber | Shocks only hurt if you are wet and cold
test: «baby eel in the zoo, tiny, can't do nothing yeah?» What does the keeper mean when he warns you? | Even young electric eels can give a tingle | Baby eels have no electric cells yet | Baby eels only bite | Baby eels give shocks only to other baby eels
test: What would happen if a hungry shark lost the jelly pores on its snout? | It would struggle to find buried fish | It could not swim straight any more, only in circles | It would go completely blind in both eyes | It would stop needing food for the rest of its life
test: A hammerhead sweeps its head over the sand like a boy with a metal detector on the beach. What is it looking for? | Stingrays hidden under the sand | Shiny coins dropped by careless divers | Patches of warm water near the bottom | Its own teeth that fell out last week
test: Count them: 3 platypuses dive in a creek. How many bills and how many legs? | 3 bills and 12 legs | 3 bills and 6 legs | 6 bills and 12 legs | 3 bills and 8 legs
test: «crap, eel just lit up the bulb on the glass, how's a fish doin that» What is the best answer? | Its body is packed with cells that act like stacked batteries | It swallowed the bulb's batteries | The keeper hid a switch in the tank and pressed it | It rubbed itself on the glass
test: Why can an elephantnose fish find worms in a river so muddy that no one could see? | It feels changes in the electric field it makes around itself | It has extra big eyes | It follows the light of glow-worms | It only ever eats in clear water
test: In a film an electric ray lies flat on the sand. A diver steps near it. What could happen? | The ray could give a strong shock to defend itself | The ray would light up like a lamp | The ray would swim up off the sand and hug the diver | Nothing, rays are made of jelly
test: «we got 2 bees, 1 flower, first bee leaves, second bee won't land. weird?» What may be going on? | The flower's charge changed after the first visit, so it may be empty | The second bee is scared of yellow | The first bee locked the flower | Bees only land on flowers nobody has touched for a year
test: Count them: 5 bumblebees sit on lavender. How many legs and how many wings? | 30 legs and 20 wings | 20 legs and 10 wings | 30 legs and 10 wings | 40 legs and 20 wings
exam: Identify the principal function of the ampullae of Lorenzini in sharks. | The detection of weak electric fields produced by other organisms | The regulation of the temperature of the blood | The reception of airborne sound | The production of electric discharges of sufficient strength to stun prey
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the foraging of the platypus beneath the water. | It locates prey by means of electric and tactile receptors in its bill | It relies principally upon acute underwater vision | It locates prey solely by its sense of smell | It detects prey by echolocation
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the taxonomic position of the electric eel? | It is a knifefish, more closely allied to catfishes than to true eels | It is a true eel of the family of freshwater eels found in European rivers | It is a species of shark | It is an amphibian of riverine habitat
exam: Ascertain the reason for which electric senses occur predominantly among aquatic animals. | Water conducts electricity far more effectively than air | Aquatic animals possess no other senses | Light is entirely absent from all waters | Electric fields cannot be generated by any animal upon dry land
